## Documentation (rustdoc)

Every **public** item needs a doc comment (`missing_docs` is denied).

### Structure

```rust
/// Load a bytecode file from bytes.
///
/// Validates the header and section table before returning a [`Module`].
///
/// # Errors
///
/// Returns [`LoadError::InvalidMagic`] if the magic is not `PHX0`.
///
/// # Panics
///
/// Never panics on malformed input.
pub fn load(bytes: &[u8]) -> Result<Module, LoadError>
```

| Section | When |
|---|---|
| Summary line | Always — what it does |
| `# Errors` | Any `Result` return |
| `# Panics` | Any panic path (prefer “never panics on …”) |
| `# Safety` | Public `unsafe` functions |
| Examples | Non-obvious safe APIs (` ``` ` fenced) |

- Comments in code explain **why**, not **what** the next line does.
- No committed `TODO` noise, prompts, or `println!` debugging.

---

## Testing Rust code

- **`#[cfg(test)]`** modules next to the code they test.
- Table-driven tests: `input`, `expected`, `name` per row.
- Test **edge cases** (empty, max length, overflow) and **error paths**, not only happy paths.
- **Arrange – Act – Assert**; one behavior per test when possible.
- Property/invariant tests for parsers and serializers when cheap to add.

---

## Before you commit Rust changes

- [ ] `cargo fmt --check`, `cargo clippy -- -D warnings`, `cargo test`
- [ ] No `unwrap`/`expect`/`dbg!` on user-facing paths
- [ ] Every `unsafe` has `// SAFETY:` and a narrow safe wrapper
- [ ] Public items documented with `# Errors` / `# Panics` / `# Safety` as needed
